# The direct pushes excused: commits that reached main with no pull request, which
# release-approval-check.sh would refuse in every release for as long as they are in its range (the
# commits since the previous published release; after the first release the baseline is no longer
# read, so a fix in a later pull request does not clear one). One commit a line: its full SHA (40
# lowercase hex digits), whitespace, and why it is excused, which is required. Comments and blank
# lines are ignored; a line written any other way stops the release. A commit listed here that is
# not in the range of the release is ignored: it was excused for an earlier one. Only a direct push
# is excused: a commit that came from a pull request is judged by that pull request's approval
# whatever this file says. The output of the check names each commit it excused, with the reason.
#
# This file is a release control. Adding a line takes a pull request, checked like any other and
# approved by a release manager who wrote none of it, who by approving it vouches for the commit
# after reading it (git show <sha>): the reason says what the commit is and why it is safe to release.
